A||||2||ChadMan||OBD-II swaps
Z||000000||ChadMan||07-30-99||08:25 AM||||What kind of unique problems (if any) would arise when swapping an OBD-II drivetrain like the SB-2 Chevy, 3800 Series II Buick, or 2.4 Twin Cam (updated Quad-4) into a Fiero?<p>My trusty Iron Duke shows no signs of dying, but when it finally expires, I'd like to replace it with a reliable late-90's powerplant.
Z||000001||squid||08-06-99||09:44 PM||||I cant say first hand but from what I have read its a pain. While the OBDII systems control the engine more accurately they are also less tolerant. I think '94 and up dont have a eprom in the computer for particular applications. Instead they rely on software. It doesnt take alot of voltage to keep the program but if you replace the battery, you could lose it and have to go to the dealer for reprograming your parameters. Stick with a 93 or older rebuilt powerplant and operating system and youll eliminate alot of potential trouble and exspense. Simpler is better in most cases. It really isnt worth acouple miles per gallon and a few horsepower. Theres always Holley Projection.
Z||000002||terryk||08-07-99||02:40 PM||||Most 95's and below still used mem-cals (some F/Y bodies had flash PCM's.<p>Also, keep in mind that 96+ engines can be run on the older ECM/PCM's. The Series II 3.8L will run on the 92-95 ECM's just fine.
